data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing the Challenge**\n\nI'm currently focused on deconstructing the initial prompt. I see it's about searching a vast space for rare, valuable items like scientific hypotheses or programs. I'm trying to pinpoint the core challenge - the needle-in-a-haystack problem, essentially, and what makes these \"items\" valuable.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 690,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 102,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "2QMDa8aTO7mio_UP45jQ6Qw"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Investigating Discovery Strategies**\n\nI'm now diving into specific questions. First, I'm comparing guided versus unguided exploration: when does one work better, and under which hypotheses? I'm also looking for theoretical results and empirical benchmarks in automated discovery. Next, I'm examining mathematical models of discovery, focusing on those tested against real data. Finally, I'm considering insights from other disciplines on the conditions for original scientific contributions.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 899,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 311,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "2QMDa8aTO7mio_UP45jQ6Qw"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Exploration Tactics**\n\nI'm now delving into specific scenarios that are highly relevant. I'm focusing on cases where disparate fields converge to form novel ideas, and on unexpressed ideas that build upon prior work, both in the context of what might boost or inhibit their appearance and success. I'm also examining optimal stopping strategies for candidate generators, and searching for the methods, hypotheses, and limitations of this type of strategy. Furthermore, I'm considering falsification tests for each previous answer, emphasizing dated observations/experiments independent of AI's output. I am planning to ensure this entire exploration is in French.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 1149,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 561,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "2QMDa8aTO7mio_UP45jQ6Qw"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Implementing Epistemic Rigor**\n\nI'm implementing strict epistemic principles. I'm structuring all responses with clear distinctions: \"**[Savoir établi]**\" for known facts, \"**[Cité de mémoire]**\" for recalled information with potential memory drift (especially numbers), and \"**[Supposition / Synthèse]**\" for extrapolations. I'm also committing to flagging any recalled numbers without a corresponding source. Counter-results will be provided when available.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 1383,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 795,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "2QMDa8aTO7mio_UP45jQ6Qw"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Detailing Theorem Application**\n\nI am now focusing on applying theorems. I'm categorizing them rigorously: general insights go under \"**[Supposition / Synthèse]**,\" while exact mathematical constants/theorems are \"**[Savoir établi]**.\" I am currently working on Section 1, which explores guided versus unguided exploration. My current thought process includes recalling theorems and conditions from authors like Wolpert & Macready, Srinivas et al., Kocsis & Szepesvári, and Auer et al.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 1678,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1090,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "2QMDa8aTO7mio_UP45jQ6Qw"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Examining Benchmarks Further**\n\nI'm now digging into empirical benchmarks related to automated discovery. I am analyzing unguided approaches that outperform or match guided ones, especially in hyperparameter optimization and neural architecture search. Specifically, I'm considering Bergstra & Bengio (2012) and Li & Talwalkar (2020), as well as looking at LLM Code/Math generation (Best-of-$N)\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 1868,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1280,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "2QMDa8aTO7mio_UP45jQ6Qw"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Comparing Sampling Techniques**\n\nI am now focusing on different sampling techniques, particularly in light of recent research. Brown et al.'s work shows independent sampling scaling well, even against guided methods, particularly when verification is easy. However, FunSearch and AlphaTensor reveal that guided approaches, such as evolutionary models and MCTS, can excel in specific contexts. I'm also considering the role of novelty search.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 2122,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1534,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "2QMDa8aTO7mio_UP45jQ6Qw"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Exploring Novelty's Impact**\n\nI'm currently thinking through empirical results like those in Lehman & Stanley (2011), which suggest that objective-driven search might be beaten by novelty search in certain situations. I'm focusing on Section 2: Mathematical Models, and diving deeper into Urn Models and Weitzman's combinatorial innovation.\n\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 2406,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1818,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "2QMDa8aTO7mio_UP45jQ6Qw"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Synthesizing Research Findings**\n\nI'm now integrating insights from economics and network science. I'm focusing on the claim that innovation is getting harder, considering supporting evidence from Bloom et al. (2020) and patent/Nobel data. I'm also analyzing network science papers by Erdős & Rényi (1960) and Uzzi et al. (2013) to better understand the connections and how they can affect scientific impact.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 2634,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2046,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "2QMDa8aTO7mio_UP45jQ6Qw"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Integrating Interdisciplinary Insights**\n\nI'm now cross-referencing insights from Web of Science analysis of 17.9M papers, particularly the significance of combining conventional and novel elements. I'm also examining branching processes and avalanches, specifically focusing on Bak, Redner, and Radicchi's models. I'm moving toward Section 3, considering philosophy of science, and exploring thinkers like Kuhn, Peirce, Popper, and Koestler.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 2929,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2341,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "2QMDa8aTO7mio_UP45jQ6Qw"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Creative Processes**\n\nI'm now examining the psychological and cognitive science aspects of creativity. I'm focusing on key works, including Wallas's stages of thought and Mednick's Remote Associates Test. I'm also considering Dunbar's findings on real-world scientific breakthroughs, and comparing these results to the traditional romantic narrative. I'm now referencing Simonton's work.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 3138,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2550,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "2QMDa8aTO7mio_UP45jQ6Qw"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Examining Scientific Creativity**\n\nI'm now diving into the neurological underpinnings of creativity and examining the work of Jung-Beeman, Beaty, and others. I'm focusing on the interplay of brain networks, including the default mode and executive control networks, during insightful problem-solving. Furthermore, I'm integrating this with Simonton's work and my prior explorations.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 3384,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2796,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "2QMDa8aTO7mio_UP45jQ6Qw"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Investigating Mutation Theory**\n\nI'm now integrating insights from evolutionary biology. I've been reviewing Luria & Delbrück's 1943 fluctuation test, a crucial study on random mutations, to set a biological baseline for unguided generation. Furthermore, I'm analyzing Fauconnier & Turner's conceptual blending theory to provide a qualitative/structural analysis. I'm now transitioning to section 4, examining structural holes in disconnected fields, as well as the latent consequences of published work.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 3616,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 3028,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "2QMDa8aTO7mio_UP45jQ6Qw"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Assessing Cross-Field Formalisms**\n\nI'm now focusing on how shared mathematical formalisms boost cross-field insights. I see examples like statistical mechanics in neural networks. Interdisciplinary hubs and dense vector embeddings also look promising. Conversely, jargon barriers, institutional silos, and translation costs all decrease the likelihood of valuable connections.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 3829,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 3241,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "2QMDa8aTO7mio_UP45jQ6Qw"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Assessing Research Barriers**\n\nI'm now focusing on section 5, particularly on techniques to address the combinatorial explosion inherent in deductive trees and the challenge of \"sleeping beauties\" and the \"file-drawer\" problem that limits the potential for discovery. I am examining statistical estimators to determine the amount of remaining knowledge and resource allocation and stopping. I am recalling the work of Turing and Good, in estimating the unobserved, and am now thinking about how to apply these methods in this context.\n\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 4101,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 3513,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "2QMDa8aTO7mio_UP45jQ6Qw"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Estimating Missing Information**\n\nI'm now focusing on section 5 and am digging into statistical estimators to address the \"file-drawer\" problem and combinatorial explosions. I'm recalling the Good-Toulmin estimator, Efron-Thisted, and Orlitsky et al. for estimating the unobserved, and am also looking at Extreme Value Theory for estimating the maximum possible value. I'm also examining the work of Chow, Robbins, and Siegmund on optimal stopping strategies, and now I'm considering their implications.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 4379,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 3791,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "2QMDa8aTO7mio_UP45jQ6Qw"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Applying Stopping Conditions**\n\nI'm now integrating the implications of optimal stopping strategies, focusing on extending these ideas. I'm recalling the theorem that $\\mathbb{E}[\\max(0, V_{n+1} - V^*)] \\le c$ and I am focused on the applications. I'm also now analyzing Gittins Index and Rotting Bandits to explore generators whose yield of novel high-value items decays with sampling, and evaluating when to stop looking. I am now working on Section 6, regarding refutation of hypotheses.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 4647,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 4059,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "2QMDa8aTO7mio_UP45jQ6Qw"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Designing Empirical Tests**\n\nI'm now focusing on designing concrete empirical tests for each of the five sections. I'm prioritizing non-AI, dated experiments. I've formulated a test for Section 1, comparing guided versus unguided search on a low-dimensional problem and an unstructured pre-image search. I'm also working on testing Section 2 through patent or compound data and analyzing growth rates. Furthermore, I'm developing experiments to test the conditions of discovery. I'm focusing on identifying the simplest and cheapest test from the set.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 4852,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 4264,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "2QMDa8aTO7mio_UP45jQ6Qw"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Designing Experimental Validations**\n\nI'm now formulating empirical tests for sections 4 and 5. For section 4, I'm considering Swanson's ABC historical splits to measure the impact of distant field pairings versus structural formalisms. Additionally, I plan to analyze the relationship between zero-citation papers with high inferential depth and their follow-up impact. For section 5, I'm thinking of drawing $10,000$ programs from a context-free grammar and evaluating deviation in new valid equivalence classes.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 588,"totalTokenCount": 5119,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 588}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 4531,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "2QMDa8aTO7mio_UP45jQ6Qw"}

